# MCO Wizard - Visual SNLP Generator
# Styles for the MCO Wizard implementation

@style Modern Minimalist Interface
>Design the MCO Wizard with a clean, minimalist aesthetic that emphasizes content and functionality. The interface should:
>
>1. Use ample white space to create visual breathing room and reduce cognitive load
>2. Implement a limited, harmonious color palette with 2-3 primary colors and strategic accent colors
>3. Utilize subtle shadows and elevation to create depth without overwhelming the interface
>4. Employ consistent, simple iconography that communicates function at a glance
>5. Use typography that prioritizes readability with a clear hierarchy (no more than 3 font sizes)
>6. Incorporate subtle animations and transitions that provide feedback without distraction
>7. Maintain consistent padding, margins, and alignment throughout the application
>
>The visual design should feel contemporary and professional while remaining timeless rather than trendy.

@style Intuitive Information Architecture
>Structure the application's information and navigation to create an intuitive, discoverable experience. The information architecture should:
>
>1. Use a clear visual hierarchy that guides users' attention to the most important elements first
>2. Group related functions and information together with visual containment
>3. Implement progressive disclosure to reveal complexity only when needed
>4. Provide clear, consistent navigation patterns that help users maintain context
>5. Use breadcrumbs and other wayfinding elements to help users understand their location
>6. Ensure that the interface is scannable with distinct sections and clear headings
>7. Balance density and breathing room to create a sense of calm focus
>
>The goal is to make the application feel intuitive even to first-time users, with each element exactly where users expect to find it.

@style Responsive Fluidity
>Create a fluid, responsive experience that adapts gracefully to different devices and contexts. The responsive design should:
>
>1. Implement a flexible grid system that reorganizes content based on available space
>2. Ensure touch targets are appropriately sized for both mouse and touch interaction
>3. Adapt navigation patterns appropriately for different screen sizes
>4. Maintain readability of text at all viewport sizes without requiring horizontal scrolling
>5. Use progressive enhancement to provide optimal experiences across device capabilities
>6. Ensure that all interactive elements provide appropriate feedback across devices
>7. Test and optimize for both portrait and landscape orientations
>
>The application should feel native and comfortable regardless of the device being used.

@style Visual Feedback System
>Develop a comprehensive visual feedback system that helps users understand the state of the application and their actions. This system should:
>
>1. Use color, animation, and iconography to indicate success, warning, and error states
>2. Provide immediate visual feedback for all user interactions
>3. Implement progress indicators for operations that take time to complete
>4. Use subtle animations to draw attention to important changes or updates
>5. Ensure that all interactive elements have clear hover, focus, and active states
>6. Use consistent visual patterns to indicate similar types of feedback
>7. Implement toast notifications for system events that don't require immediate action
>
>The feedback system should help users feel confident and in control while using the application.

@style Accessibility-First Design
>Ensure that the application is accessible to all users by implementing an accessibility-first approach. The accessible design should:
>
>1. Maintain sufficient color contrast ratios that exceed WCAG AA standards
>2. Implement proper semantic HTML structure for screen reader compatibility
>3. Ensure all interactive elements are keyboard accessible with visible focus states
>4. Provide text alternatives for all non-text content
>5. Design forms with clear labels and error messages
>6. Allow users to customize text size and contrast settings
>7. Test regularly with assistive technologies to ensure compatibility
>
>Accessibility should be treated as a fundamental design principle, not an afterthought.

@style Consistent Component Language
>Create a cohesive design system with consistent components that create a unified experience. The component language should:
>
>1. Establish a library of reusable UI components with consistent behavior and appearance
>2. Implement consistent interaction patterns across similar components
>3. Use visual variations systematically to indicate different component states or importance
>4. Maintain consistent spacing relationships between components
>5. Ensure that related components share visual characteristics
>6. Document component usage guidelines to maintain consistency as the application evolves
>7. Balance variety and consistency to create interest without confusion
>
>The component language should make the application feel like a cohesive whole rather than a collection of disparate parts.

@style Delightful Micro-Interactions
>Incorporate thoughtful micro-interactions that add moments of delight without sacrificing usability. These micro-interactions should:
>
>1. Provide subtle animation feedback that reinforces user actions
>2. Use playful elements sparingly to celebrate achievements or completion
>3. Implement satisfying transitions between states and pages
>4. Add personality through small, unexpected details that don't distract from the task
>5. Use sound (when appropriate and user-enabled) to enhance important feedback
>6. Ensure that all animations and interactions have purpose beyond decoration
>7. Allow users to reduce or disable animations if preferred
>
>These micro-interactions should make the experience feel polished and considered while adding moments of joy to the workflow creation process.
